<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<!--
  R004 fixed — the stray end event is removed.
  Repair chosen: delete end_delivered_copy. Unlike the R003 orphan, this one
  carries no recoverable intent — the outcome it names is already reachable
  through end_delivered, so connecting it would only add a redundant path.

  The alternative a model reaches for — connect "Deliver pizza" to the orphan —
  is a trap, confirmed empirically 2026-07-21. It clears R004 and leaves the
  model tier-1 clean and tier-2 sound, while giving the task two unconditional
  outgoing flows. Under BPMN 2.0 that is an uncontrolled split and both are
  taken. With the earlier "Order cancelled" wording the two branches were
  mutually exclusive outcomes, so the process ended in contradictory states;
  with the duplicate the damage is milder (a redundant token) but the structural
  defect is the same. Tier 2 cannot see either, because pm4py translates an
  uncontrolled split into a free choice in the Petri net (one exit place feeding
  two transitions) rather than a parallel one. Only tier 3 catches it. Fixing it
  properly needs a gateway, not a condition — conditional flows out of an
  activity are evaluated independently, so two conditions neither guarantee
  exclusivity nor guarantee that either fires.

  Note: R004 has no registered quick fix — it was removed 2026-07-21, the mirror
  of the R003 removal. Had it been "repaired" to always return a source instead,
  it would have produced exactly the trap described above.
  Expected: none.
-->
<definitions xmlns="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/20100524/MODEL"
             id="def_R004" targetNamespace="http://bpmn.io/schema/bpmn">
  <process id="proc_R004" name="Pizza order (stray end event)" isExecutable="false">
    <startEvent id="start_order" name="Order received"><outgoing>f_ordered</outgoing></startEvent>
    <task id="task_bake" name="Bake pizza"><incoming>f_ordered</incoming><outgoing>f_baked</outgoing></task>
    <task id="task_deliver" name="Deliver pizza"><incoming>f_baked</incoming><outgoing>f_delivered</outgoing></task>
    <endEvent id="end_delivered" name="Pizza delivered"><incoming>f_delivered</incoming></endEvent>
    <sequenceFlow id="f_ordered" sourceRef="start_order" targetRef="task_bake"/>
    <sequenceFlow id="f_baked" sourceRef="task_bake" targetRef="task_deliver"/>
    <sequenceFlow id="f_delivered" sourceRef="task_deliver" targetRef="end_delivered"/>
  </process>
</definitions>
